<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cuzcatlan</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1528 CE (approx.) · capital Cuzcatlan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Nahua-speaking Pipil in western and central El Salvador, which resisted Spanish conquest from 1524 to 1528.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> descended from Nahua-speaking migrants from central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> who reached the Pacific lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> in waves between about 900 and 1200. They settled what is now <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and founded a number of lordships, with Cuzcatlan, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiguo-cuscatlan">Antiguo Cuscatlán</a>, the most powerful.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cuzcatlan was ruled by a lord and nobles, with priests and warriors at the top of society. Its rich volcanic soils produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, the most valuable trade crop in the region, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and balsam, traded widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> religion and calendar resembled those of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1524 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> invaded and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acajutla">Acajutla</a> and Tacuzcalco, but they resisted for years; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, first founded in 1525, was re-established in 1528. The territory became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-guatemala">Captaincy General of Guatemala</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cuzcatlan.jpg" alt="Cuzcatlan" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Juan Miguel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poblaciones_cabecera_Altepetl_Se%C3%B1orio_de_Cuzcatlan_1524.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1528 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cuzcatlan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Pipil religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nawat</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Señorío de Cuzcatlán, Pipil kingdom of Cuzcatlan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=cuzcatlan" aria-label="Cuzcatlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2235 1669 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cuzcatlan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2555 1925L4910 989M2555 1925L2518 1892"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2518 1892h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2555 1925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2555 1925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=cuzcatlan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
